(1) Preliminary plat. Any person seeking approval of a proposed exempt subdivision plat shall submit such plat directly to the City of Winder Planning and Development Department a plat which has been prepared in accordance with subsection (2) of this section, together with the prescribed fee.
a. Preliminary plats shall be submitted to the City of Winder Planning and Development Department. After determining that the application is complete, the application shall be distributed internally to the relevant reviewing departments for assessment and approval.
b. Once a preliminary plat has been reviewed internally by staff, staff will schedule and present the planning, zoning, and appeals board's recommendation to the city council during a public hearing.
c. At the public hearing, city council will evaluate both the proposed plat and the planning, zoning, and appeals board's recommendation. The council may decide to approve the recording of the plat as requested, or they may return it for necessary corrections or amendments, clearly specifying the required changes in writing.
(2) Final plat. A developer/subdivider shall submit a final plat to the City of Winder Planning and Development Department pursuant to the following sequence of submittal, review, and approval:
a. A minimum of three (3) copies of the final plat shall be submitted.
b. Approval procedures. After the public hearing, submittal of the final plat and all supporting documentation, data, and certifications, the final plat shall be approved or disapproved by the City of Winder Planning and Development Department which shall be in conformity with the previously approved preliminary plat. The City of Winder Planning and Development Department may disapprove any such final plat if any portion of the proposed subdivision is in violation of any applicable ordinance, law, rule, or regulation of any governmental unit having jurisdiction.
c. Completeness. If any of the specifications or facts required herein are omitted or misrepresented, the City of Winder Planning and Development Department may refuse to review such a platform and may return such submittal to the developer/subdivider for completion or revision.
d. Final plat specifications. The final plat shall be prepared by a Georgia Registered Land Surveyor. If a professional engineer, licensed to practice in Georgia, shall contribute to the plat, then appropriate seals shall be shown. The plat shall be drawn at a scale appropriate to show details but no smaller than two hundred (200) feet to one (1) inch. The plat shall be drawn in permanent ink on reproducible material upon a sheet or sheets not exceeding seventeen (17) by twenty-two (22) inches, in compliance with Georgia law, and unless specifically waived, in whole or in part by the City of Winder Planning and Development Department, shall include:
1. The name of the subdivision and road names;
2. The name, address, seal, and license number of the registered land surveyor; the original signature of the surveyor shall be across the seal for the plat to be valid; also, if a professional engineer participated in the development of the plat, the name, address, seal, and license number of such professional engineer shall be placed thereon;
3. The date of the plat drawing, graphic scale, north point, notation as to the reference of bearings to magnetic, true north, or grid north and indication whether bearings shown are calculated from angles turned or taken from compass readings;
4. The location of the parcel (land district and land lot) giving total acreage being subdivided, total number of lots created and total length of new roads within the development;
5. A general location map at a scale not less than one (1) inch equals one thousand (1,000) feet, showing the relationship of the plat to other existing roads and properties in City of Winder;
6. The index map where more than one (1) sheet is required to present the plat; [Note: multiple sheets shall each be named to conform with the title sheet and designated for example: 1 of 3, 2 of 3, and 3 of 3];
7. Identify the point of beginning (POB) tied to the nearest existing road intersection or nearest land lot line or district line;
8. Exact boundary lines and zoning classification(s) of the parcel shall be in compliance with the Georgia Plat Act, O.C.G.A. § 15-6-67;
9. City, county, or land lot lines accurately tied to the lines of the subdivision by distance and angles when such lines traverse the subdivision;
10. Existing road centerlines, showing angles of deflection and standard curve data of intersection, radii, length of tangents and arcs, and degree of curvature;
11. Lot lines with dimensions to the nearest 1/100 foot, and bearings to the nearest minute, arcs, and chords and tangent to radii of rounded corners;
12. The location and dimension of all approved exempted access easements;
13. Front and rear structure or building setback lines; side setback lines may be noted upon the survey, but shall not be required to be shown upon each lot;
14. Lots or parcels numbered in numerical order;
15. Locations of monuments and markers to the degree of accuracy required of professional engineers and/or registered land surveyors pursuant to Georgia law;
16. The boundary and elevation of the 100-year floodplain as determined by F.I.R.M. and/or best available data, as determined by the city engineer;
17. Applicable certificates and statements as specified in this chapter;
18. The location and dimension of all areas proposed to be set aside for recreational use, open space, green space, or other public use, with designation of the purpose thereof, and conditions, if any, of the dedication or reservation;
19. The Barrow County Tax Assessor's map and existing parcel numbers for G.I.S. purposes.
20. If an exempt subdivision proposes access to a state highway, GDOT shall determine the feasibility of such access pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 32-6-151. The subdivider/developer shall produce permits and/or written permission from GDOT for the proposed access.
21. If an exempt subdivision proposes access to a Barrow County highway, the Barrow County Public Works Department shall determine the feasibility of such access. The subdivider/developer shall produce permits and/or written permission from the Barrow County Public Works Department for the proposed access.
